RK27xx ZSP500 DSP test suite
============================

The tests that established how the RK27xx's ZSP500 DSP works: instruction
set and flags, timers, interrupts and low-power modes, addressing, the
external bus window, mailbox, DMA, and timing. They run on the device under
hwstub, driven by ../zsp.lua. What they established is written up in the
ZSP500 DSP programming manual:
https://www.rockbox.org/wiki/pub/Main/Rockchip27xx/ZSP500_DSP_Programming_Manual.pdf
The DSP simulator's timing model (tools/zsp/zspsim.py) is fitted to their
cycle benchmarks and bus and DMA timing.

  tests/        hand-written tests (*.s), generators of the others
                (gen_*.py), shared includes (*.inc), and the result checker
                (expect.py)
  tools/        gen_bench.py (cycle benchmarks), bench_report.py,
                bus_sweep.py (bus and DMA timing against the clocks)
  Makefile      builds the test images with tools/zsp/zspasm.pl
  results/      written on the device by zsp.lua (not in git)

Requirements: perl and Python 3 on the host, hwstub_shell
(utils/hwstub/tools), an RK27xx player running the hwstub stub
(utils/hwstub/stub/rk27xx).

All commands below start in utils/hwstub/tools, which is also where
hwstub_shell has to run so that the lua/... paths resolve.


1. Build
--------

    make                              # hwstub_shell
    make -C lua/rk27xx/zsp            # the test images, tests/*.bin
    make -C lua/rk27xx/zsp bench      # the cycle benchmarks, tests/bench/*.bin

Generated sources, images and listings are not in git; make clean removes
them.


2. Functional tests (inside ./hwstub_shell)
-------------------------------------------

    dofile("lua/rk27xx/zsp.lua")
    ZSP.setup()
    ZSP.run_group("all")
    ZSP.restore()

"all" runs the groups startup, isa, timers, risky, irq, irq2, smode, misc,
addr, bus, bus2 and bus3 in that order (ZSP.GROUPS in zsp.lua says what each
covers); ZSP.run_group(name) runs one, ZSP.run(test) one test. Each test
writes results/<test>.txt, apart from the bring-up probes of the startup
group (t0a, t0b, t0d-t0g): they run for a fixed time and print what they
left in DMEM, as each one's header describes. The risky group may hang the
DSP, not the host: the DSP is reset before every test. Some tests need the
ARM during the run (NMI, mailbox, SDRAM, DMA); their groups call the helpers
for that.


3. Cycle benchmarks
-------------------

    ZSP.setup()
    ZSP.bench(ZSP.BENCH)                          -- single instruction kinds
    ZSP.bench(ZSP.BENCH_MIX, { append = true })   -- groups from the MP3 synthesis
    ZSP.restore()

They take a few minutes and write results/bench.txt. Each repeats an
instruction group in a hardware loop, timed with the ARM's TMR1 at the DSP
clock hwstub leaves (160 MHz).


4. Bus and DMA timing
---------------------

The bus and DMA tests count in DSP cycles; run at several DSP clocks they
show which part of each cost is DSP cycles and which is the AHB/SDRAM clock
(HCLK). At hwstub's HCLK, 100 MHz (ARM 200 MHz, as Rockbox boosted):

    ZSP.sweep()
    ZSP.restore()
    os.execute("mv lua/rk27xx/zsp/results/sweep lua/rk27xx/zsp/results/sweep-hclk100")

At Rockbox's unboosted clocks, ARM 50, HCLK 50 MHz. clk.lua switches the ARM
clocks with the register writes of set_cpu_frequency(); zsp.lua is loaded
again after the switch so that ZSP.restore() puts back the switched
registers, not hwstub's:

    dofile("lua/rk27xx/clk.lua")
    CLK.set("normal")
    dofile("lua/rk27xx/zsp.lua")
    ZSP.sweep()
    ZSP.restore()
    CLK.set("hwstub")
    os.execute("mv lua/rk27xx/zsp/results/sweep lua/rk27xx/zsp/results/sweep-hclk50")

ZSP.sweep() runs t30_bustime, t35_dma and t36_dma2 at DSP clocks of 18 to
160 MHz (ZSP.SWEEP_MHZ; opts.mhz, opts.tests to change), results in
results/sweep/<MHz>/. Every result records the DSP, ARM and AHB clocks it
ran at.

Steps 2 to 4 also run unattended: ./hwstub_shell -b -e '<commands>'.


5. Checking the results (in lua/rk27xx/zsp)
-------------------------------------------

    make results                                       # every test: pass/fail
    python3 tools/bench_report.py                      # cycles per instruction group
    python3 tools/bus_sweep.py results/sweep-hclk100   # cycles = fixed + time part
    python3 tools/bus_sweep.py results/sweep-hclk50
    python3 ../../../../../../tools/zsp/zspbench.py    # zspsim.py against the benchmarks

make results reports every check of tests/expect.py; on a Samsung YP-CP3
all pass. INFO lines carry measured values. tests/expect.py RESULTDIR
checks another directory, e.g. one sweep step.


Also
----

zsp_clk.lua runs tclk_count in each DSP clock mode (PLL settings, bypass,
slow mode) and measures the clock it gets:

    dofile("lua/rk27xx/zsp.lua"); dofile("lua/rk27xx/zsp_clk.lua")
    ZSPCLK.run()

Each mode is announced before it is set: if the device stops answering,
that mode hung the bus.

Writing a test: a source in tests/ that includes common.inc and
prologue.inc (a7 = result block) and ends with epilogue.inc (dumps the
registers and writes the magic the runner waits for). Add it to the
Makefile, a checker to tests/expect.py and the test to a group in
ZSP.GROUPS (zsp.lua).
